        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "- Finally, since the book's authorship has become more collective, I have somewhat wistfully decided to retire what Gregory G. Colomb, who edited the tenth edition, called Joe's \"ubiquitous I's.\" This change is not inconsequential, for in choosing to use I, Williams was embracing the struggles of ordinary writers as his own. But with this edition, it just seemed too artificial for me to put my words directly into his mouth. Still, despite the dropping of the first-person singular, the book continues to be animated by this basic solidarity with its readers.",
